UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 3, Lesson 1: Dutch and other early visitors to Australia', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Dutch and other early European visitors reached parts of the Australian coastline before the British colony of 1788. Encounters with First Nations Peoples, when recorded, must be read carefully as one-sided written sources; oral histories held by Communities are not ours to invent.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhy must we treat a European journal of first contact as a limited source?\n\nFEEDBACK\nIt shows one side’s view, may misunderstand First Nations actions and leaves out Community knowledge not written there.\n\nWATCH FOR\nRetelling contact as a friendly adventure story without First Nations perspectives hides conflict, confusion and unequal power in the records.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nUsing two published sources, complete a contact card: European group, region in general terms, date range, what the written source claims and one question you would ask a First Nations educator. No invented dialogue.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Dutch and other early European visitors reached parts of the Australian coastline before the British colony of 1788. Encounters with First Nations Peoples, when recorded, must be read carefully as one-sided written sources; oral histories held by Communities are not ours to invent.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-HIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Using two published sources, complete a contact card: European group, region in general terms, date range, what the written source claims and one question you would ask a First Nations educator. No invented dialogue. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: It shows one side’s view, may misunderstand First Nations actions and leaves out Community knowledge not written there. Reasoning: I used Dutch by attending to visitor rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Retelling contact as a friendly adventure story without First Nations perspectives hides conflict, confusion and unequal power in the records. Correction: It shows one side’s view, may misunderstand First Nations actions and leaves out Community knowledge not written there. Why: the correction restores accurate use of Dutch.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Retelling contact as a friendly adventure story without First Nations perspectives hides conflict, confusion and unequal power in the records. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating Dutch on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with Dutch, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"It shows one side’s view, may misunderstand First Nations actions and leaves out Community knowledge not written there. This matches the lesson skill for Dutch.","options":["It shows one side’s view, may misunderstand First Nations actions and leaves out Community knowledge not written there.","Retelling contact as a friendly adventure story without First Nations perspectives hides conflict, confusion and unequal power in the records.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Copy the model places and call that independent work."],"question":"Why must we treat a European journal of first contact as a limited source?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Retelling contact as a friendly adventure story without First Nations perspectives hides conflict, confusion and unequal power in the records. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of visitor with clear evidence.","Retelling contact as a friendly adventure story without First Nations perspectives hides conflict, confusion and unequal power in the records.","Dutch and other early European visitors reached parts of the Australian coastline before the British colony of 1788. Encounters with First Nations Peoples,…","It shows one side’s view, may misunderstand First Nations actions and leaves out Community knowledge not written there."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising Dutch?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named visitor, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the visitor used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source."],"question":"A learner is practising Dutch. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"It shows one side’s view, may misunderstand First Nations actions and leaves out Community knowledge not written there. uses sources and places appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Retelling contact as a friendly adventure story without First Nations perspectives hides conflict, confusion and unequal power in the records.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Copy the model places and call that independent work.","It shows one side’s view, may misunderstand First Nations actions and leaves out Community knowledge not written there."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about Dutch?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Retelling contact as a friendly adventure story without First Nations perspectives hides conflict, confusion and unequal power in the records.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about Dutch.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about visitor?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Example: a class uses a published school source about a Dutch voyage along a western or northern coast (as named in that source). They…","Retelling contact as a friendly adventure story without First Nations perspectives hides conflict, confusion and unequal power in the records."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Dutch and other early European visitors reached parts of the Australian coastline before the British colony… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Retelling contact as a friendly adventure story without First Nations perspectives hides conflict, confusion and unequal power in the records.","Using two published sources, complete a contact card: European group, region in general terms, date range, what the written source claims and one…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the model places and call that independent work."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of Dutch?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating Dutch on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: It shows one side’s view, may misunderstand First Nations actions and leaves out Community knowledge not written there. Reasoning: I used Dutch by attending to visitor rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with Dutch, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Retelling contact as a friendly adventure story without First Nations perspectives hides conflict, confusion and unequal power in the records. Correction: It shows one side’s view, may misunderstand First Nations actions and leaves out Community knowledge not written there. Why: the correction restores accurate use of Dutch.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y4-w03-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise Dutch.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"History","success_criteria":["I can use Dutch with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify visitor and explain why it matters.","I can practise coast independently on a new prompt.","I can notice encounter needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Dutch and other early European visitors reached parts of the Australian coastline before the British colony of 1788. Encounters with First Nations Peoples, when recorded, must be read carefully as one-sided written sources; oral histories held by Communities are not ours to…","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Dutch and other early European visitors reached parts of the Australian coastline before the British colony of 1788. Encounters with First Nations Peoples, when recorded, must be read carefully as one-sided written sources; oral histories held by Communities are not ours to invent.","A claim unrelated to the source or place."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about Dutch?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a class uses a published school source about a Dutch voyage along a western or northern coast (as named in that source). They record ship, approximate date and that the land was already occupied. They do not invent dialogue or Dreaming interpretations.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWhy must we treat a European journal of first contact as a limited source?\n\nFEEDBACK\nIt shows one side’s view, may misunderstand First Nations actions and leaves out Community knowledge not written there.","explain":"Dutch and other early European visitors reached parts of the Australian coastline before the British colony of 1788. Encounters with First Nations Peoples, when recorded, must be read carefully as one-sided written sources; oral histories held by Communities are not ours to invent.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Retelling contact as a friendly adventure story without First Nations perspectives hides conflict, confusion and unequal power in the records. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Early coastal visitors.","caption":"No invented dialogue.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Early visitors</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Dutch and others</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Limit</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">one-sided journals</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example: a class uses a published school source about a Dutch voyage along a western or northern coast (as named in that Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example: a class uses a published school source about a Dutch voyage along a western or northern coast (as named in that Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about Dutch?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a class uses a published school source about a Dutch voyage along a western or northern coast (as named in that source). They record ship, approximate date and that the land was already occupied. They do not invent dialogue or Dreaming interpretations. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Limited European record.","caption":"Two sources.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Contact card</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">questions for educator</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">adventure-only story</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about Dutch?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nRetelling contact as a friendly adventure story without First Nations perspectives hides conflict, confusion and unequal power in the records. Here is a more productive model: Example: a class uses a published school source about a Dutch voyage along a western or northern coast (as named in that source). They record ship, approximate date and that the land was already occupied. They do not invent dialogue or Dreaming interpretations.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nIt shows one side’s view, may misunderstand First Nations actions and leaves out Community knowledge not written there.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Retelling contact as a friendly adventure story without First Nations perspectives hides conflict, confusion and unequal power in the records. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about Dutch?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDutch and other early European visitors reached parts of the Australian coastline before the British colony of 1788. Encounters with First Nations Peoples, when recorded, must be read carefully as one-sided written sources; oral histories held by Communities are not ours to invent. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Transfer to a new inquiry"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 3, Lesson 1: Dutch and other early visitors to Australia","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"First contacts and exploration","week":3,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: a class uses a published school source about a Dutch voyage along a western or northern coast (as named in that source). They record ship, approximate date and that the land was already occupied. They do not invent dialogue or Dreaming interpretations.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the sources, places or community details used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Early coastal visitors.","caption":"No invented dialogue.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Early visitors</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Dutch and others</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Limit</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">one-sided journals</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Limited European record.","caption":"Two sources.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Contact card</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">questions for educator</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">adventure-only story</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 4"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-hsie-y4-w03-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
